    "Beta blockers may be reasonable for patients 42 years or older with recurrent vasovagal syncope (VVS). Efficacy for younger patients is not well supported according to the mentioned studies. The effectiveness of salt and fluid intake for VVS is limited but may be encouraged unless contraindicated, particularly for patients with a history of hypertension, renal disease, or cardiac dysfunction. In patients with recurrent VVS, a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor like fluoxetine or paroxetine may be considered, though evidence of effectiveness is contradictory. In selected patients, reducing or withdrawing medications that cause hypotension may be considered when appropriate.": "",
